 "name": "Which Street Panorama Is More Attractive? (Chongqing Yuzhong)",
 "description": "Method replication: participants see two 360° street panoramas (unrolled into wide strips, one above the other) from Yuzhong District, Chongqing, and answer the paper's question, \"Which image is more attractive?\", choosing either image or \"Indistinguishable\", for 30 pairs. The intro lists the paper's four aesthetic dimensions (composition and visual balance, colour, spatial layering and architectural detail, overall visual appeal). Choices score +1 / 0 / −1 as in the paper. The images are not the paper's stimuli (40 Baidu Street View panoramas of Yuzhong, 2017–2023, cropped above the capture car).\nOriginal study: Zhou, Zhang & Zhu (2025), \"Evaluating Urban Visual Attractiveness Perception Using Multimodal Large Language Model and Street View Images\", Buildings 15(16), 2970. From 8,000 Baidu panoramas of Yuzhong (2017–2022), 100 images were each compared with 50 random others by human raters with the same three answers, with repeated pairs used to drop inconsistent judgements; GPT-4o answered the same pairwise question for 1,000 images, and its scores were compared with the human scores and Place Pulse 2.0 predictions and related to space syntax measures.\nDifferences from the original study: (1) 30 pairs per participant from 40 new panoramas instead of 50 comparisons for each of 100 images; (2) no repeated pairs for a consistency check; (3) the bottom of each panorama is cut off to hide the capture car; (4) age group and gender are asked.",
